		"meta": {
			"author": "",
			"category": "effect",
			"deploymentTarget": {
				"branch": "",
				"name": "Wire",
				"version": {
					"major": 7,
					"minor": 23,
					"patch": 0
				}
			},
			"description": "Fire and Smoke - effect: flames and smoke from one simplified fluid simulation (advection, rising heat, wind, swirls; plausible rather than exact), half resolution, 32-bit float. Smoke rises out of the flames, wind blows both, smoke dissolves after its life time. Flame 0 and Smoke 0 = everything dies out and the image stands still. Sources (combine freely): Bottom \/ Top \/ Left \/ Right On = burning screen edges; Edge From \/ To = part of each edge that burns (0-1 along the edge); Gaps (%) + Seed = irregular, patchy ember line. Point On \/ X \/ Y \/ Size = a single fire place (0 = centre, -1 \/ 1 = screen edge). Mask On = the clip under this effect is the burn mask: everything brighter than Mask Level burns (logo, text, silhouette). Show Input = keep the clip visible under the fire. Fire: Flame and Smoke = strength; Flame Height = how far flames reach before cooling down; Smoke Life (s) = time until smoke dissolves; Rise = upward draft; Wind (deg) = direction the wind blows to (0 = right, 90 = up) and Wind Strength; Turbulence = swirls and licking tongues; Flare = trigger, every rising edge over 0.5 makes the fire flare up for a moment (map a kick or button). Colors: Flame Core \/ Mid \/ Tip from hottest to coolest, Smoke Color. Note: speed follows the frame rate. Shaders FireSim.fs and FireRender.fs must stay next to the patch.",
			"displayName": "Fire and Smoke",
			"identifier": "a2119064-ca03-44d0-83fd-baa40f8bf23e",
			"licenseName": "",
			"mail": "",
